#hasheq((status . "current") (milestone . 896) (wave . "W1") (issue . 9724) (branch . "campaign/v1.00.31-w1") (implementation-sha . "47591b3069700f6ba264991d67242c6904da28ae") (content-digest . "38edfb195f7e8ae025046421802c6d3f0ee86d1c7b91c66fce53c54730ffce42") (planning-sync . "current") (review-artifact . "docs/reports/gsd-wave-reviews/v1.00.31-w1.rktd") (red-first . #hasheq((command . "racket scripts/ci/compiled-root.rkt build --out \"$stage/q-compiled/trusted-root\" --checkout \"$PWD\" --trusted-label q-trusted-producer ; racket scripts/run-tests.rkt --suite workflows") (failure . "executed against the unmerged W4 head ce13d0388b56b850d67fa50f7ca5bea093581e62 the declared invocation exited 1 with stderr 'compiled-root: unknown switch: --out', so the only existing guard - a substring assertion that the action file contains the invocation text - was satisfied by a declaration that could never run (artifact: artifacts/wave-delivery-integrity/v1.00.31-w1/raw/w4-producer-repro-prefix.txt). Three further reds were observed live during this wave and are the reason its content head moved three times: (a) the two new tests declared '@suite workflows' while the workflows suite selected 29 files without them, because workflows-file? matched on path only and ignored the tag every sibling area predicate honours - the tag selected nothing; (b) the first F1 register guard reported 'refused whenever the live declaration verified, i.e. it reported refusal exactly when nothing was refused and reported 'not-refused while catching the defect, so it could not have failed for the reason the register's contract states; (c) the second git fixture added by this wave ran `git init` without sanitizing the inherited git plumbing, so under the pre-commit hook's full mode it could have re-initialised the canonical checkout as bare a second time (the incident that corrupted the checkout during this wave). (a) and (b) were found by the round-1 independent review, (c) by the round-2 review; all three were fixed before this record."))) (focused-tests . #hasheq((command . "raco test tests/test-ci-runtime-contract.rkt tests/test-w9-ci-workflow-verification.rkt tests/test-milestone-gate.rkt tests/test-run-tests.rkt tests/test-wave-delivery-integrity-register.rkt tests/test-workflow-invocation-contract.rkt tests/test-compiled-root-workflow.rkt tests/test-compiled-root.rkt") (detail . "all touched and related files pass at the final head, 0 failures. Includes the eight checks that moved with the wave: the invocation-contract tests both repository-wide and against the extracted declaration; the register harness (F1 guarded-pass, every other row unguarded with its reproduction-liveness check, and the falsification assertion that hands the F1 guard the pre-fix declaration and observes 'not-refused); the classifier regression test asserting the workflows suite selects both new tests and not the tagged helper; the milestone gate's v1.00.29-w0 ownership-matrix drift check (green after canonical regeneration); the two re-pinned CI-contract tests (checkpoint file hash bcdbc2c6, prepare_action_sha256 608d93c5); and the git fixture, which passes in direct AND simulated-hook modes (11/11 each) with the hook's GIT_DIR leaked at a DECOY repository, after which core.bare is still false on both the decoy and the canonical checkout and HEAD is unchanged.") (result . "passed"))) (format-compile . #hasheq((command . "raco fmt -i scripts/run-tests/classify-filters.rkt scripts/ci/compiled-root.rkt scripts/ci/invocation-contract.rkt tests/test-run-tests.rkt tests/test-wave-delivery-integrity-register.rkt tests/test-ci-runtime-contract.rkt tests/test-workflow-invocation-contract.rkt tests/test-compiled-root-workflow.rkt tests/test-compiled-root.rkt ; raco make <same>") (detail . "raco fmt produced no diff and raco make is clean on every touched Racket module; each edit was applied through the Racket-aware editor, which formats and compiles (and reverts on failure) - recorded in the pre-commit hook output of the content commits.") (result . "passed"))) (lint . #hasheq((command . "racket scripts/check-deps.rkt ; racket scripts/metrics.rkt --lint ; racket scripts/tier-ownership-matrix.rkt check ; racket scripts/run-tests/inventory.rkt --ownership-map --check artifacts/tier-ownership/v1.00.29-w0/ownership-matrix.json ; sha256sum -c <the three touched SHA256SUMS>") (detail . "all external packages declared in info.rkt; all 5 static metrics match README.md (Test files 1504, Source modules 892, Source lines 188384, Test lines 277327, Test assertions 41971); tier-ownership matrix green (999 rows); the versioned v1.00.29-w0 ownership matrix passes the canonical drift check ('PASS: matrix matches reality') after being regenerated by the canonical generator inventory.rkt --ownership-map --tier-matrix with its sibling SHA256SUMS refreshed (65b79131 -> d3f04c56); the touched checksum files verify 6/6, 1/1 and 1/1 (artifact dirs verify from the repo root, since their entries carry repo-root-relative paths). The repository 18-check CI lint passed at this head.") (result . "passed"))) (fast . #hasheq((command . "racket scripts/run-tests.rkt --suite fast") (detail . "PASS at head 92ca8db0: files=1203 pass=1203 fail=0 timeout=0 skip=0; tests=17909 pass=17909 fail=0; wall-clock-seconds=960.029. The only difference between that head and this record's implementation-sha is a documentation correction in this wave's own report (git diff 92ca8db0..47591b30 is exactly one .md file, and nothing executes docs), so the measurement stands for the final head; the focused set (210 tests) and the workflows suite (31 files / 180 tests, 40.4 s) were both re-run at the final head together with the lint gates. The wave's declared suite also passes on the same tree and really contains the new tests: it selected 29 files before the classifier fix, plus the whole-checkout producer executed end to end against this repository: exit 0, 2386 modules compiled, 10 non-module .rkt inputs skipped and counted, 269 s, read-only publication, no staging leftovers, digest 2b9f03498e37c909aa9b9b57c5025f6b8d25c0292fd57485d570b8b3da7bc338.") (result . "passed"))) (workflows . #hasheq((command . "racket scripts/run-tests.rkt --suite workflows") (detail . "files=31 pass=31 fail=0 timeout=0 skip=0; tests=180 pass=180 fail=0; metadata-completeness=explicit:31/heuristic:0/missing:0. Both new tests are selected by the suite they declare: test-compiled-root-workflow.rkt (executes the extracted declaration, asserts fail-closed shapes) and test-workflow-invocation-contract.rkt (contracts all 87 declarations in the repository). The classifier change is covered by tests/test-run-tests.rkt, which asserts the routing rather than trusting it.") (result . "passed"))) (remaining-items . (#hasheq((classification . "deferred-noncritical") (owner . "W6 owner (adversarial permanence rehearsal)") (rationale . "Task 2 asked to ban substring-only invocation assertions. The one offender is replaced and the repository-wide contract makes such an assertion worthless (it can no longer pass while the invocation is broken), but the ban is not mechanical: a newly written substring assertion would still be inert on its own. Enforced is the contract, not the syntax of assertions; a lint over assertion shapes belongs with W6's adversarial permanence work.")) #hasheq((classification . "deferred-noncritical") (owner . "W4 owner (delivery-tool API contract)") (rationale . "The delivery ladder's governance stage cannot self-resolve its merged binding PR because resolve_prs_for_branch filters head=<slug>:<branch> where the GitHub API needs head=<owner>:<branch> (register F6). Confirmed live on W0's binding PR; repairing the resolver is W4's subject, so this wave leaves the stage untouched.")) #hasheq((classification . "deferred-noncritical") (owner . "W4/W7 owners (guarded compiled-root activation)") (rationale . "The producer publishes into the already-allowlisted q-compiled/ prefix, and restore-racket-environment/action.yml mirrors every compiled/ directory it finds under q-compiled/ - which on activation would also match the nested q-compiled/trusted-root/**/compiled and copy it to spurious checkout paths. Inert while the lane's input default ('false') is not overridden, and no workflow passes it 'true'; the activation wave must narrow that find or move the root out of the mirrored prefix. Found by the round-2 review.")) #hasheq((classification . "noncritical") (owner . "W2 owner (evidence identity and digest integrity)") (rationale . "The compiled-root manifest's sources list is harvested from every .zo under the checkout (excluding docs/) whose sibling source exists, not from the derived module list, so a real-tree run reports 2389 sources against 2386 compiled modules; the extras are bytecode an earlier raco make left in the tree. Mechanism identified from collect-checkout-compilation-pairs and named in the report, but no gate compares the two counts. Harmless for identity (every entry must still resolve to an in-checkout source).")) #hasheq((classification . "noncritical") (owner . "next legitimate plan revision (candidate owner W2)") (rationale . "Metadata-window invisibility: a test whose @speed/@suite tags sit beyond the parser's 30-line window is invisible to the tier-ownership matrix even though the runner still executes it (this wave hit it once and moved the tags). It is a governance-visibility gap, not a correctness hole. Registering it would change the frozen plan body and force a rebind of in-flight waves' trios, so it is recorded in the report rather than as a register row.")) #hasheq((classification . "noncritical") (owner . "hardening owner (not this wave)") (rationale . "checkout-compilation-clean? compares git's stdout and ignores its exit code, so a git that failed before printing would read as clean. The function predates this wave and the live dirty-input refusal shows it works in practice; carried forward rather than changed inside an invocation-contract wave. Found by the round-2 review.")) #hasheq((classification . "noncritical") (owner . "W2 owner (evidence identity and digest integrity), for reconciliation in the next legitimate plan revision") (rationale . "The wave document's acceptance clause asks that each declared invocation be executed or matched exactly to the script's parsed option set; 44 of 87 declarations are matched at the recorded weak tier instead, because their targets parse options by hand rather than declaring a command-line table (scripts/run-tests.rkt is one, and it is CI-critical). The strong tier is used wherever a static command-line table exists, every tier is emitted per declaration, and 0 targets are unverifiable, so no declaration ships unchecked - but the literal clause is stronger than a static checker can deliver for those 44. Raised by the round-3 review; accepted and recorded rather than smoothed over, and reconciled explicitly in the review artifact's coordinator decision.")) #hasheq((classification . "deferred-noncritical") (owner . "W5 owner (artifact provenance and determinism)") (rationale . "scripts/gsd-wave-gate.rkt receives the content digest as a caller-supplied --content-digest value and only checks that the record agrees with what it was handed, so the chain 'recompute the digest from the diff' lives outside the gate that enforces it. This wave did recompute it independently from the base..head diff before writing the records (base 6c602681 pinned at PR creation), but the enforcement point is a discipline rather than a mechanism - the F1 shape one level up. Binding the gate to the diff it is validating is W5's subject.")))))